{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"11970ac0177689b6","name":"card_unit_18e_regiment","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/units/card_unit_18e_regiment","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"18e RÉGIMENT","text":"On turn end, set operation cost to 0 if this was the only unit you deployed.","flavorText":"The 18th Infantry Regiment is a unit of the French Army formed in 1791 during the French Revolution, from a French royal regiment dating back to 1587. It took part in the American Revolutionary War, notably in the decisive siege of Yorktown in 1781. It also participated in numerous battles during the Napoleonic Wars and it fought on all major French battlefields during World War I: the Marne, the Aisne River, the Somme and Verdun. When World War II broke out, the regiment, which was part of the 36th Infantry Division, was transferred to the Maginot Line, in the Moselle area. In September and October 1939, at the beginning of the “Phoney War”, it was involved in the Saar Offensive, a French military operation in German territory, launched in response to the German invasion of Poland. In May and June 1940, during the Battle of France, the Division fought in the French Ardennes at Attigny, repelling the German assaults for 25 days, despite being outnumbered and outgunned. The 18th Regiment lost a third of its men during those fierce combats, with 300 dead and 700 wounded. The regiment and several of its members were decorated for their bravery during this battle. After the Armistice, the 18th Regiment was reformed as part of the Armistice Army, but it was disbanded in November 1942 following Operation Anton, the German invasion of Vichy France. Many officers and soldiers of the 18th then joined the “maquis” (the underground) and formed the Pommiès Free Corps, leading resistance operations in all South-West of France as part of the Army Resistance Organization, until June 1944. They later took part in the general insurrection and the liberation of France with the French Forces of the Interior, until they were incorporated in the French Army in February 1945 after the liberation of Alsace, and named 49th Infantry Regiment. The 49th Regiment participated in the crossing of the Rhine and the capture of Stuttgart, where it ended the war.","faction":"France","type":"infantry","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":3,"operationCost":3,"attack":4,"defense":4},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_18e_regiment","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_18e_regiment_title","SourceString":"18e RÉGIMENT","LocalizedString":"18e RÉGIMENT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::infantry","attack":4,"defense":4,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::France","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_18e_regiment_text","SourceString":"On turn end, set operation cost to 0 if this was the only unit you deployed.","LocalizedString":"On turn end, set operation cost to 0 if this was the only unit you deployed."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_18e_regiment_history","SourceString":"The 18th Infantry Regiment is a unit of the French Army formed in 1791 during the French Revolution, from a French royal regiment dating back to 1587. It took part in the American Revolutionary War, notably in the decisive siege of Yorktown in 1781. It also participated in numerous battles during the Napoleonic Wars and it fought on all major French battlefields during World War I: the Marne, the Aisne River, the Somme and Verdun. When World War II broke out, the regiment, which was part of the 36th Infantry Division, was transferred to the Maginot Line, in the Moselle area. In September and October 1939, at the beginning of the “Phoney War”, it was involved in the Saar Offensive, a French military operation in German territory, launched in response to the German invasion of Poland. In May and June 1940, during the Battle of France, the Division fought in the French Ardennes at Attigny, repelling the German assaults for 25 days, despite being outnumbered and outgunned. The 18th Regiment lost a third of its men during those fierce combats, with 300 dead and 700 wounded. The regiment and several of its members were decorated for their bravery during this battle. After the Armistice, the 18th Regiment was reformed as part of the Armistice Army, but it was disbanded in November 1942 following Operation Anton, the German invasion of Vichy France. Many officers and soldiers of the 18th then joined the “maquis” (the underground) and formed the Pommiès Free Corps, leading resistance operations in all South-West of France as part of the Army Resistance Organization, until June 1944. They later took part in the general insurrection and the liberation of France with the French Forces of the Interior, until they were incorporated in the French Army in February 1945 after the liberation of Alsace, and named 49th Infantry Regiment. The 49th Regiment participated in the crossing of the Rhine and the capture of Stuttgart, where it ended the war.","LocalizedString":"The 18th Infantry Regiment is a unit of the French Army formed in 1791 during the French Revolution, from a French royal regiment dating back to 1587. It took part in the American Revolutionary War, notably in the decisive siege of Yorktown in 1781. It also participated in numerous battles during the Napoleonic Wars and it fought on all major French battlefields during World War I: the Marne, the Aisne River, the Somme and Verdun. When World War II broke out, the regiment, which was part of the 36th Infantry Division, was transferred to the Maginot Line, in the Moselle area. In September and October 1939, at the beginning of the “Phoney War”, it was involved in the Saar Offensive, a French military operation in German territory, launched in response to the German invasion of Poland. In May and June 1940, during the Battle of France, the Division fought in the French Ardennes at Attigny, repelling the German assaults for 25 days, despite being outnumbered and outgunned. The 18th Regiment lost a third of its men during those fierce combats, with 300 dead and 700 wounded. The regiment and several of its members were decorated for their bravery during this battle. After the Armistice, the 18th Regiment was reformed as part of the Armistice Army, but it was disbanded in November 1942 following Operation Anton, the German invasion of Vichy France. Many officers and soldiers of the 18th then joined the “maquis” (the underground) and formed the Pommiès Free Corps, leading resistance operations in all South-West of France as part of the Army Resistance Organization, until June 1944. They later took part in the general insurrection and the liberation of France with the French Forces of the Interior, until they were incorporated in the French Army in February 1945 after the liberation of Alsace, and named 49th Infantry Regiment. The 49th Regiment participated in the crossing of the Rhine and the capture of Stuttgart, where it ended the war."},"operationCost":3,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BloodAndIron","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"hasGuard":true,"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/France/Mini_Ally_Expansion_25/t_18e_regiment.t_18e_regiment","SubPathString":""},"artist":"French WWII Poster","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/18e_REGIMENT.18e_REGIMENT","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":313.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_18e_regiment_photo","SourceString":"Members of the Pommiès Free Corps during the liberation of Tarbes, August 1944.","LocalizedString":"Members of the Pommiès Free Corps during the liberation of Tarbes, August 1944."},"blowUpExplosionNiagara":{"ObjectName":"NiagaraSystem'NS_ExplosionInfantryM'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Niagara/NS_ExplosionInfantryM.46"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedC